WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.674
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.674 --> 00:00:05.817
Hi Dora.

00:00:05.817 --> 00:00:10.107
&gt;&gt; Hello, hi everyone,
good to see you, how nice is this for

00:00:10.107 --> 00:00:12.350
us to be here in person.

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:13.440
&gt;&gt; Welcome back to Stanford.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.250
&gt;&gt; Thank you, so glad to be here.

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:19.782
&gt;&gt; As you can see, we're all really
excited and grateful to have you here,

00:00:19.782 --> 00:00:21.655
but truly no one more than me.

00:00:21.655 --> 00:00:26.121
You might not remember this, but exactly
two years ago, I logged into a Zoom and

00:00:26.121 --> 00:00:28.000
you were my alumni interviewer.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:28.868
&gt;&gt; I remember, I do remember.

00:00:28.868 --> 00:00:33.751
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK] For GSB admissions, I
don't know what lies you put down on that

00:00:33.751 --> 00:00:36.408
paper but you've really-
&gt;&gt; I speak the truth.

00:00:36.408 --> 00:00:40.675
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] On multiple levels,
you've really made this moment happen, so

00:00:40.675 --> 00:00:41.395
thank you.

00:00:41.395 --> 00:00:44.839
&gt;&gt; Thank you, I'm so
glad to see you here and thriving.

00:00:44.839 --> 00:00:49.453
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So then and now, so when you
interviewed me when I was entering the GSB

00:00:49.453 --> 00:00:54.508
or applying for the GSB and now, as I was
preparing to interview you, I reached out

00:00:54.508 --> 00:00:59.437
to a lot of friends in the industry and
I asked them, what do you know about Dora?

00:00:59.437 --> 00:01:03.860
because I wanted to get some insights and
some intel, and the thing that kept coming

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:08.902
back, there are three words, ambitious,
incredibly brilliant, and really humble.

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:12.352
And I'd love to kind of go back to
the beginning because I think it seemed as

00:01:12.352 --> 00:01:14.635
though when people
articulated that about you,

00:01:14.635 --> 00:01:17.480
they're speaking to
the truth of your being.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:21.817
A lot of that's formed when you're young,
in your childhood, in your upbringing.

00:01:21.817 --> 00:01:25.078
So I'd love to know,
where does your ambition come from, and

00:01:25.078 --> 00:01:29.270
really what creates that humility
that people are so endeared towards?

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.967
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you, thank you so
much for saying that.

00:01:31.967 --> 00:01:36.539
I have to give a lot of credit to my mom,
because growing up, I was that kid,

00:01:36.539 --> 00:01:41.184
I always dreamed big, that kid that
you're like, my gosh, stop, but

00:01:41.184 --> 00:01:44.112
I was always so excited,
I always dreamed big.

00:01:44.112 --> 00:01:47.813
And my mom would say to me,
Dora, I like your ambition, but

00:01:47.813 --> 00:01:51.010
don't forget to have
ambition with contentment.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:55.324
And that was something that meant so much
to me, and it's been my North Star right

00:01:55.324 --> 00:01:58.630
from when I was a child right up
until now, and always will be.

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:04.441
When I think about that, it makes me
remember, no matter how high you are,

00:02:04.441 --> 00:02:07.665
be humble, and
no matter how low you are, be hopeful.

00:02:07.665 --> 00:02:12.644
Because things are never, you're never,
at least I'll say I'm never as amazing

00:02:12.644 --> 00:02:16.610
as I think I am, and I'm never as
terrible as I think I am, right?

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:19.943
There's all that space in between and
so for me,

00:02:19.943 --> 00:02:23.940
it's been about how can I make
as much impact in the world?

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.545
My ambition is not a selfish
ambition just about me, for me,

00:02:27.545 --> 00:02:30.050
it's about what it represents.

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:34.564
I feel, there not a lot of black women
that get to great stages like this or

00:02:34.564 --> 00:02:39.180
be in positions like that and for
me, I'm trying to demystify that.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.469
My hope is that, black women who
are coming beside me, behind me,

00:02:43.469 --> 00:02:47.451
can look into, she can do it,
I can definitely do it, and

00:02:47.451 --> 00:02:52.380
that can kind of infuse them and inspire
them to go and achieve great things.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:57.016
And I'd love for, we need a world where
more people in positions of power reflect

00:02:57.016 --> 00:03:02.520
the population, and so I think about how
I can contribute to that in a small way.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.203
&gt;&gt; Well, early in your career,
you had a humbling moment,

00:03:06.203 --> 00:03:09.910
you described your career as
a failed career in finance.

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:13.078
And you were just telling us a story in
the back about how that all kind of came

00:03:13.078 --> 00:03:15.358
to being that you've started
a career in marketing.

00:03:15.358 --> 00:03:17.843
So I'd love for
you to share that story with us now and

00:03:17.843 --> 00:03:21.135
then we'll talk a little bit more
about how you transitioned since.

00:03:21.135 --> 00:03:24.555
&gt;&gt; Of course, I'll keep it real
because it's the GSB, right?

00:03:24.555 --> 00:03:26.990
[LAUGH] So
I was going through a rotational program,

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:30.277
one of those programs where you
rotate through investment banking,

00:03:30.277 --> 00:03:33.697
investment research, investment
management and sales and trading.

00:03:33.697 --> 00:03:35.837
And I had my heart set
on investment banking,

00:03:35.837 --> 00:03:38.612
because that was the thing that
everyone was doing at the time.

00:03:38.612 --> 00:03:41.892
And I was from Nigeria,
I didn't really know,

00:03:41.892 --> 00:03:46.648
I remember when I actually stumbled into,
I walked into a place where

00:03:46.648 --> 00:03:51.000
there had free food,
college students to pack some free food.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.767
And I show up in my yoga clothes, everyone
is in a suit, because this is actually

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:57.543
a recruiting event for
Goldman Sachs, believe it or not.

00:03:57.543 --> 00:04:01.170
[LAUGH] And as I'm about to quickly leave
the room, this man walks up to me and

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.945
he's a partner and
he's having a conversation with me.

00:04:03.945 --> 00:04:07.814
And he starts just asking me, he's like,
well, clearly you didn't plan to be here,

00:04:07.814 --> 00:04:09.091
and I was like, not really.

00:04:09.091 --> 00:04:11.444
And he starts having
a conversation with me.

00:04:11.444 --> 00:04:12.948
I tell him about what I'm doing,

00:04:12.948 --> 00:04:16.939
I'm leading a fashion show at Harvard
at the time called Eleganza, the other.

00:04:16.939 --> 00:04:20.659
And he was like, well, you seem
very entrepreneurial, very driven,

00:04:20.659 --> 00:04:24.009
this is a production with a lot of people,
that's management.

00:04:24.009 --> 00:04:26.287
Have you thought about getting into
finance, and I was like, not really.

00:04:26.287 --> 00:04:29.196
And he gave me his card and
he was like, contact me, and I did.

00:04:29.196 --> 00:04:31.731
And I ended up getting an internship,
that's how I fell into.

00:04:31.731 --> 00:04:34.445
So it wasn't so
much of something I had calculated,

00:04:34.445 --> 00:04:36.490
I was just kind of following the motion.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:41.249
And as I was going through this program,
this partner said to me, he said,

00:04:41.249 --> 00:04:46.340
you're good at investment banking but
you're great at marketing.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:49.006
And I remember when he first said that,
I was like,

00:04:49.006 --> 00:04:53.260
I don't know why I'm not quite taking
this compliment as a compliment.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:57.651
And I think it's because, he was telling
me, okay, this thing you're okay at but

00:04:57.651 --> 00:04:59.937
this other thing you're really great at.

00:04:59.937 --> 00:05:04.430
And when I took a step back and
I reflected on it, I realized it was true.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:08.200
I love the data driven
analytical aspect of finance,

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.960
but I love storytelling,
I love being creative.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:13.234
And for me marketing,

00:05:13.234 --> 00:05:17.590
I blossomed there because that allowed
me to combine my passion, right, for

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:22.610
data with my passion for telling stories
and kind of bring those things together.

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:25.430
So yeah, once he said that, I've been
in marketing pretty much ever since.

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:28.777
I doubt this man even knows who I am.

00:05:28.777 --> 00:05:31.945
And I think that's a reminder,
when you're in a position of leadership,

00:05:31.945 --> 00:05:35.415
the impact you can have on people's lives
by the things that you say or don't say,

00:05:35.415 --> 00:05:36.640
and I think about that a lot.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:41.832
&gt;&gt; One of the jokes that got said
about millennials is that we

00:05:41.832 --> 00:05:46.165
can't keep a job for more than two years,
present company included.

00:05:46.165 --> 00:05:51.256
And I think there's a lot of emphasis
that's placed on staying power, so

00:05:51.256 --> 00:05:56.362
being able to stay with a company for
decades, an entire lifetime, etc.

00:05:56.362 --> 00:05:59.951
One of the things that's really remarkable
about your career is it seems to me that

00:05:59.951 --> 00:06:02.982
you've always known when it's time
to either stay or when to leave.

00:06:02.982 --> 00:06:06.962
You always kind of make a transition at
what appears to be the right moments.

00:06:06.962 --> 00:06:11.304
And so I'm curious to know kind of how
do you assess opportunities as they

00:06:11.304 --> 00:06:14.078
come your way or
the ones that you seek out, and

00:06:14.078 --> 00:06:18.375
what are some of the principles that
guide you in that decision making?

00:06:18.375 --> 00:06:21.566
&gt;&gt; So, one of the things, I guess
the largest principle that guides me is

00:06:21.566 --> 00:06:24.590
the ambition with contentment
which I've talked about.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:28.480
But I also think about how I chase
opportunities that create other

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:29.547
opportunities.

00:06:29.547 --> 00:06:34.079
I ask myself,
am I able to bloom where I'm planted or

00:06:34.079 --> 00:06:37.479
is this a time where I need to go to grow?

00:06:37.479 --> 00:06:41.387
And for me, if I'm still learning,
and I'm still growing,

00:06:41.387 --> 00:06:45.384
then I need to stay where I'm at and
I need to continue to bloom.

00:06:45.384 --> 00:06:49.268
But if the sunlight's gone,
and I'm not learning anymore,

00:06:49.268 --> 00:06:51.830
it might be time to go to grow.

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:56.553
So I try to focus on that, not so
much how much money am I going to make, or

00:06:56.553 --> 00:07:00.068
is this company doing better
at this point in time?

00:07:00.068 --> 00:07:04.358
But I try more to focus on,
where can I bloom, where can I thrive,

00:07:04.358 --> 00:07:07.790
where can I contribute
the most that I possibly can?

00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:10.898
And for me, it's all about learning,
I'm a lifelong learner.

00:07:10.898 --> 00:07:15.284
And so I think as long as I'm learning, as
long as I'm able to contribute to the best

00:07:15.284 --> 00:07:18.681
of my ability, then I'm going to
stay there and I'm going to bloom.

00:07:18.681 --> 00:07:23.046
But when the sunlight's gone, I'm going to
say goodbye, I'm going to go to grow.

00:07:23.046 --> 00:07:26.002
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] One of the things I've really
admired about you is how intentional

00:07:26.002 --> 00:07:28.002
you've been about
professional development.

00:07:28.002 --> 00:07:31.072
I think here and
just maybe in the Bay Area, more broadly,

00:07:31.072 --> 00:07:32.888
where there are a lot of startups,

00:07:32.888 --> 00:07:36.917
sometimes leadership is framed as
something that happens spontaneously.

00:07:36.917 --> 00:07:40.510
You're walking around one day,
you suddenly have a great idea.

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:43.073
You then build a 5,000 people company, and

00:07:43.073 --> 00:07:46.774
suddenly you're in a crisis and
you have to lead through that crisis.

00:07:46.774 --> 00:07:49.362
But you've been really,
really intentional, and so

00:07:49.362 --> 00:07:52.972
I want to understand a little bit more
where that intentionality comes from.

00:07:52.972 --> 00:07:56.741
And also through your experience in
developing yourself as a leader, so

00:07:56.741 --> 00:08:00.666
coming to a place like the GSB,
seeking out an executive coach, etc.

00:08:00.666 --> 00:08:03.700
What have you learned about
yourself as a leader?

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:06.758
&gt;&gt; So how many of you have taken
Touchy Feely, or going to take it,

00:08:06.758 --> 00:08:08.096
interpersonal dynamics?

00:08:08.096 --> 00:08:09.424
I see some hands in the room.

00:08:09.424 --> 00:08:13.701
I love that class so much, it was a very
painful class, but I love it very much.

00:08:13.701 --> 00:08:17.669
There's how you see yourself,
there's how other people see you, and

00:08:17.669 --> 00:08:20.196
there's how you think
other people see you.

00:08:20.196 --> 00:08:23.855
And sometimes there could be
a discrepancy between those three things.

00:08:23.855 --> 00:08:28.987
And I think one of the things that I
learned at Stanford in this interpersonal

00:08:28.987 --> 00:08:34.359
dynamics class was about the importance
of how to bring those things together,

00:08:34.359 --> 00:08:38.154
right, and create less of a gap and
a distance, right?

00:08:38.154 --> 00:08:40.072
because you don't want there
to be that much distance.

00:08:40.072 --> 00:08:43.906
There's always going to be some distance,
because that's just the reality of life,

00:08:43.906 --> 00:08:45.375
but how can you bridge that gap?

00:08:45.375 --> 00:08:48.011
And for me, I really focused and

00:08:48.011 --> 00:08:53.784
spend a lot of time thinking about
the impact that I can have on others.

00:08:53.784 --> 00:08:58.383
And really thinking and
trying to be quite intentional about how,

00:08:58.383 --> 00:09:02.322
I can't control what you do,
but I can control what I do,

00:09:02.322 --> 00:09:05.298
and I can control how I
react to what you do.

00:09:05.298 --> 00:09:09.850
And just really placing emphasis
on controlling what I'm able to

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:14.744
control is what I kind of leaned into and
what I've really focused on.

00:09:14.744 --> 00:09:19.226
&gt;&gt; I'm switching gears a little
bit to your career in marketing.

00:09:19.226 --> 00:09:22.947
The business environment right now
just seems incredibly volatile, for

00:09:22.947 --> 00:09:25.755
lack of a better word, and I just think-
&gt;&gt; That is a good word.

00:09:25.755 --> 00:09:29.038
&gt;&gt; Yeah, [LAUGH] I think
the role of a marketing team,

00:09:29.038 --> 00:09:32.490
the role of a CMO has become so
critical at this time.

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:34.275
It feels like a really exciting time, but

00:09:34.275 --> 00:09:36.317
also a really terrifying
time to be a marketer.

00:09:36.317 --> 00:09:40.330
But before we begin talking about that
a little more, I would like to know from

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:43.975
you, especially knowing that at
different companies as a CMO means so

00:09:43.975 --> 00:09:47.461
many different types of functions,
types of responsibilities.

00:09:47.461 --> 00:09:51.543
How would you write your own job
description as it exists today?

00:09:51.543 --> 00:09:54.693
&gt;&gt; So I lead our global marketing
communications and membership team, and

00:09:54.693 --> 00:09:57.649
marketing kind of comprises of
every aspect of marketing, right?

00:09:57.649 --> 00:10:03.322
Product marketing, demand generation,
creative, consumer strategy and insights,

00:10:03.322 --> 00:10:08.859
and then our communications, internal
communications, external communication.

00:10:08.859 --> 00:10:11.739
A membership is really
the organization that focuses on

00:10:11.739 --> 00:10:13.709
our subscription business, right?

00:10:13.709 --> 00:10:17.782
Our membership is this, how we continue to
be a place that our members want to be at,

00:10:17.782 --> 00:10:20.982
show up at, and how we're engaging
them at the deepest levels.

00:10:20.982 --> 00:10:24.572
&gt;&gt; So I think my job is the full
spectrum of marketing, right?

00:10:24.572 --> 00:10:29.756
When I think about, how do I generate
interest and awareness, all the way to

00:10:29.756 --> 00:10:35.191
how do I capture that interest and bring
you into the fold, into the ecosystem.

00:10:35.191 --> 00:10:38.532
How do I nurture that, and then how
do I turn you into an advocate and

00:10:38.532 --> 00:10:42.193
someone who not only sticks around,
but you help bring other people in?

00:10:42.193 --> 00:10:44.570
So that's how I think about it.

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:48.671
&gt;&gt; Social media has really changed what
marketing looks like over the past

00:10:48.671 --> 00:10:49.363
few years.

00:10:49.363 --> 00:10:53.466
I think, it's created sort of this
environment in which brands, especially

00:10:53.466 --> 00:10:57.894
consumer brands, are expected to be in
conversation with their potential buyers.

00:10:57.894 --> 00:11:02.002
To always be on, to have a voice,
to have a personality at times,

00:11:02.002 --> 00:11:04.693
if we think about brands, like Wendy's or

00:11:04.693 --> 00:11:08.539
some of these others that are very,
very active on Twitter.

00:11:08.539 --> 00:11:12.396
I think as part of that,
brands have become really embedded in

00:11:12.396 --> 00:11:17.306
the sociopolitical discourse that
happens outside of these media channels.

00:11:17.306 --> 00:11:21.877
And so I'm curious about what
challenges and opportunities that this

00:11:21.877 --> 00:11:26.614
expectation has created for brands and
how you address that at Peloton?

00:11:26.614 --> 00:11:29.991
&gt;&gt; So I think consumers
want to know who you are.

00:11:29.991 --> 00:11:31.985
&gt;&gt; That's just the world
that we live in now.

00:11:31.985 --> 00:11:36.966
And I think it's really important as a
brand that you're clear on who you are and

00:11:36.966 --> 00:11:38.239
what you stand for.

00:11:38.239 --> 00:11:40.412
I think that's really important.

00:11:40.412 --> 00:11:44.465
At the same time, if you stand for
everything, you stand for nothing, right?

00:11:44.465 --> 00:11:47.720
And if you talk and
comment about everything,

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:51.068
your voice starts to
become background noise.

00:11:51.068 --> 00:11:55.119
So I think it's really important that
you're clear on what matters most to us

00:11:55.119 --> 00:11:57.876
and why,
some of you might remember that question.

00:11:57.876 --> 00:12:00.628
It's a good question for
a brand to ask itself, right?

00:12:00.628 --> 00:12:01.833
What's our purpose?

00:12:01.833 --> 00:12:03.269
Why do we exist?

00:12:03.269 --> 00:12:06.462
And then make sure that
you are standing up and

00:12:06.462 --> 00:12:10.762
having a voice in those issues
that are most relevant to you.

00:12:10.762 --> 00:12:15.630
But I like to say it to my team this way,
I'm like, we want to slay in our lane, but

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:16.662
there is a lane.

00:12:16.662 --> 00:12:20.946
Think about what happens if you're driving
in a highway and everyone is just going,

00:12:20.946 --> 00:12:22.163
that is chaos, right?

00:12:22.163 --> 00:12:25.794
And I think as a brand, you can get into a
lot of trouble when you forget about that,

00:12:25.794 --> 00:12:28.498
because it's like, wait,
why are you talking about this?

00:12:28.498 --> 00:12:33.856
And it almost can be a distraction
to your actual purpose and mission.

00:12:33.856 --> 00:12:38.313
So I always say, we want to be
purpose-led not cause-led, right?

00:12:38.313 --> 00:12:40.459
Because when we're cause-led,
then we're all over the place.

00:12:40.459 --> 00:12:43.155
When we're purpose-led,
we're showing up for

00:12:43.155 --> 00:12:47.211
causes that are in line with why we exist,
what matters most to us, right?

00:12:47.211 --> 00:12:50.679
And at Peloton, we're about improving
people's lives through fitness.

00:12:50.679 --> 00:12:54.073
So whenever there are things that are in
that space, we want to show up there,

00:12:54.073 --> 00:12:55.358
we want to have a voice there.

00:12:55.358 --> 00:13:00.051
But if it's not directly connected to that
issue, it doesn't mean we're not going to

00:13:00.051 --> 00:13:04.826
take stances to help our employees and our
team members and different stakeholders.

00:13:04.826 --> 00:13:09.726
But you might not see us necessarily
being the loudest voice in the room,

00:13:09.726 --> 00:13:13.743
because I firmly believe if
you're not going to take action,

00:13:13.743 --> 00:13:16.491
you shouldn't speak, I believe that.

00:13:16.491 --> 00:13:17.630
Why?

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:22.413
Because you're taking space of people
who are actually taking action.

00:13:22.413 --> 00:13:25.485
And I think it's performative, and
I don't think we should do that.

00:13:25.485 --> 00:13:28.603
I think if you're actually going to
speak about an issue, take action.

00:13:28.603 --> 00:13:31.869
If you don't have an action you can take,
maybe you should allow

00:13:31.869 --> 00:13:35.016
[LAUGH] the people who are actually
doing something about it for

00:13:35.016 --> 00:13:37.338
the attention of
the spotlight to be on them.

00:13:37.338 --> 00:13:41.554
So that you're not taking space away and
energy away from where the momentum is,

00:13:41.554 --> 00:13:44.102
and I think that's really
important as a brand.

00:13:44.102 --> 00:13:48.880
And then the last thing I'll say on this
issue, as you can see I'm very passionate

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:52.638
about it, is that I think things
like this need to be inside out.

00:13:52.638 --> 00:13:53.890
What do I mean by that?

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:56.848
You have to first start,
charity begins at home.

00:13:56.848 --> 00:14:01.239
You need to make sure that you're doing
the work internally before you start

00:14:01.239 --> 00:14:03.310
talking about things externally.

00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:06.876
Now, you will not be perfect,
no brand is perfect,

00:14:06.876 --> 00:14:11.267
because you will never get 100%
of everything done internally.

00:14:11.267 --> 00:14:13.365
But the work must begin internally.

00:14:13.365 --> 00:14:17.394
If you start talking about things
externally, that you're not doing anything

00:14:17.394 --> 00:14:21.182
about internally, that is actually,
I think, going to backfiring on you

00:14:21.182 --> 00:14:24.752
as a brand, and it can put you in hot
water and we've seen that happen.

00:14:24.752 --> 00:14:29.775
&gt;&gt; How do you balance that desire to be
a kind of personality through a brand or

00:14:29.775 --> 00:14:34.960
for a brand to be personable with also
the need to be able to grow your market or

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:36.758
grow your customer base.

00:14:36.758 --> 00:14:42.623
So how do you balance doing so without
alienating potentially new customers?

00:14:42.623 --> 00:14:47.365
&gt;&gt; I think, that's a really good question,
and that's why I talk about your lane and

00:14:47.365 --> 00:14:48.943
when you're slinging it.

00:14:48.943 --> 00:14:51.852
I might not agree with you,
but I can respect you.

00:14:51.852 --> 00:14:52.641
Do you know what I mean?

00:14:52.641 --> 00:14:55.553
There are people you don't always
agree with, but you're like,

00:14:55.553 --> 00:14:57.177
that's a thoughtful perspective.

00:14:57.177 --> 00:15:00.288
I have a different point of view on this,
but I respect who you are,

00:15:00.288 --> 00:15:04.364
I respect that you're authentic, I respect
that you stand up for what you believe in.

00:15:04.364 --> 00:15:09.102
What people do not like is what they
perceive as being performative or

00:15:09.102 --> 00:15:10.252
hypocritical.

00:15:10.252 --> 00:15:13.438
Those are the two things that I think
really alienate and separate you as

00:15:13.438 --> 00:15:16.960
a brand from your consumer, when they
feel like you're not being authentic.

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:19.929
So you're just telling me what
you think I want to hear,

00:15:19.929 --> 00:15:22.846
you're not necessarily
saying what you believe in.

00:15:22.846 --> 00:15:26.002
Or this doesn't really align
with how you normally show up.

00:15:26.002 --> 00:15:28.923
So I'm asking questions,
question mark, question mark.

00:15:28.923 --> 00:15:33.133
And I think that the best thing you can
do is how can you be authentic to your

00:15:33.133 --> 00:15:33.761
purpose?

00:15:33.761 --> 00:15:35.790
How can you be consistent about it?

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:39.154
And how can you make sure that
anything that you're speaking about or

00:15:39.154 --> 00:15:41.880
you're talking about,
you've action to back it up and

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:45.196
you started the work internally
before you started going outside?

00:15:45.196 --> 00:15:48.459
&gt;&gt; So you joined Peloton
at a really exciting time.

00:15:48.459 --> 00:15:51.135
The company was experiencing
a lot of growth.

00:15:51.135 --> 00:15:55.210
If you walked by the Presidential Center
at 6 PM any given night,

00:15:55.210 --> 00:15:59.150
you could see people in their
little windows doing a class.

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:00.548
And now-
&gt;&gt; It's great.

00:16:00.548 --> 00:16:01.915
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Thank you all for

00:16:01.915 --> 00:16:03.950
being Peloton members, it's awesome.

00:16:03.950 --> 00:16:08.549
&gt;&gt; And now things have changed at Peloton,
from the industry standpoint, or

00:16:08.549 --> 00:16:13.100
from the news standpoint,
is said to be struggling as a business.

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:16.567
What are some of the things that you
believe that you will have to get right in

00:16:16.567 --> 00:16:18.329
your role knowing that in marketing,

00:16:18.329 --> 00:16:20.714
that growth is sometimes
a function of marketing?

00:16:20.714 --> 00:16:23.383
What do you believe you have
to get right in your role for

00:16:23.383 --> 00:16:26.430
this moment to be able to
continue to grow at Peloton?

00:16:26.430 --> 00:16:30.734
&gt;&gt; So I'll start with what has changed,
and what is the same, right?

00:16:30.734 --> 00:16:32.930
because I think that's important context.

00:16:32.930 --> 00:16:36.950
So what has changed is
the macroeconomic climate has changed.

00:16:36.950 --> 00:16:38.410
That has changed, right?

00:16:38.410 --> 00:16:39.624
There was a pandemic.

00:16:39.624 --> 00:16:41.450
[LAUGH] I don't know if you all remember.

00:16:41.450 --> 00:16:44.387
We were all stuck indoors,
and now that's changed.

00:16:44.387 --> 00:16:46.660
We're in a different context.

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:49.350
So the macroeconomic context has changed.

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:54.227
What has stayed the same is that people
still want to be motivated to lead

00:16:54.227 --> 00:16:56.290
a healthy, happy life.

00:16:56.290 --> 00:17:00.701
People still want an exercise option,
an option to move their body and

00:17:00.701 --> 00:17:03.690
care for
the mind that is going to inspire them,

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:07.170
that is going to make
them want to show up.

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:12.004
And one of the things that I think is
amazing about Peloton is that in spite of

00:17:12.004 --> 00:17:16.931
everything that it's been through,
and we've been through some things.

00:17:16.931 --> 00:17:18.051
It's been a wild ride, y'all [LAUGH].

00:17:18.051 --> 00:17:20.514
But in spite of everything,
our members continue to show up.

00:17:20.514 --> 00:17:25.236
And that is because our focus on
improving the lives of our members, and

00:17:25.236 --> 00:17:29.800
our focus on our member experience
continues to remain true.

00:17:29.800 --> 00:17:31.205
That continues to be our true north.

00:17:31.205 --> 00:17:33.210
That continues to be our north star.

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:35.962
And I think that it's
important to remember that,

00:17:35.962 --> 00:17:39.994
because I think if we take our eye off
doing what's right for our members and

00:17:39.994 --> 00:17:44.358
putting them first, We can't do that.

00:17:44.358 --> 00:17:48.202
So I think as a business, we've gotta
make sure that first and foremost,

00:17:48.202 --> 00:17:49.930
we are serving our members.

00:17:49.930 --> 00:17:53.662
We are making sure that we're providing
them with an engaging experience that they

00:17:53.662 --> 00:17:54.905
want to show up to every day.

00:17:54.905 --> 00:17:56.250
That's job number one.

00:17:56.250 --> 00:18:00.322
And as a marketer, I need to make sure
that my organization's doing everything

00:18:00.322 --> 00:18:03.079
that we can to support our members and
put them first.

00:18:03.079 --> 00:18:06.418
And then I think the other thing that
we're doing is we're making sure that

00:18:06.418 --> 00:18:09.601
people understand who we are and
what we do, and what we offer, right?

00:18:09.601 --> 00:18:14.273
When we first started out, Peloton, and
I give all the credit to our incredible

00:18:14.273 --> 00:18:18.458
founders who found this out to be true
before the whole world caught up,

00:18:18.458 --> 00:18:21.670
right, this idea of connected fitness,
right?

00:18:21.670 --> 00:18:24.718
I can get an engaging workout
from the comfort of my home.

00:18:24.718 --> 00:18:28.330
This wasn't really something people
thought could happen, and they built this.

00:18:28.330 --> 00:18:31.260
They created this category,
which I think is amazing.

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:34.820
And now, as the world is evolving,

00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:41.100
how do we show how that continues to
expand in this new macroeconomic context?

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:44.437
So we have to show that yes,
we were relevant in the pandemic, but

00:18:44.437 --> 00:18:45.902
we're still relevant now.

00:18:45.902 --> 00:18:49.459
That motivating incredible experience
that we provide you hasn't gone away.

00:18:49.459 --> 00:18:53.312
And I think we've got the Peloton bike,
we've got the Peloton but

00:18:53.312 --> 00:18:56.215
we also have the Peloton app,
which has outdoor running,

00:18:56.215 --> 00:19:00.120
has a variety of exercises, so
you don't have to only use it in the home.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:01.388
You can use it in the gym.

00:19:01.388 --> 00:19:02.570
You can use it on the go.

00:19:02.570 --> 00:19:07.507
And so being able to really tell that
story of how Peloton is really helping to

00:19:07.507 --> 00:19:12.135
drive and help people stay fit,
be motivated not only in one space, but

00:19:12.135 --> 00:19:15.631
in multiple spaces,
that omnichannel experience.

00:19:15.631 --> 00:19:17.100
I think that's really important.

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:21.891
That's something as marketers,
that's job number two.

00:19:21.891 --> 00:19:25.947
&gt;&gt; So looking internally, how are you
leading the team that you had, and

00:19:25.947 --> 00:19:28.630
then also thinking about
the executive team as

00:19:28.630 --> 00:19:31.400
well through this critical
inflection point?

00:19:34.190 --> 00:19:38.739
&gt;&gt; Leadership, I think it's so funny,
because I think [LAUGH] everyone's like,

00:19:38.739 --> 00:19:39.615
leadership.

00:19:39.615 --> 00:19:41.350
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Welcome to my TED Talk.

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:43.658
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I think it's so

00:19:43.658 --> 00:19:47.640
easy to think you're a good leader
when everything's going great.

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:48.530
Do you know what I mean?

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:50.904
It's like, I'm a great leader.

00:19:50.904 --> 00:19:54.130
Well, maybe it's just
the macroeconomic context.

00:19:54.130 --> 00:19:58.377
I think it's harder to lead
when things are tough, right?

00:19:58.377 --> 00:20:01.310
And I think that's really where
who you are shows up, and

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:04.540
the impact you can have on
other people's lives show.

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:08.707
And I think my number one thing
is I'm a people first leader.

00:20:08.707 --> 00:20:10.229
I believe that to the core of who I am.

00:20:10.229 --> 00:20:13.264
I treat people how I want to be treated,
and

00:20:13.264 --> 00:20:17.790
that is fundamental to me in my life,
and in my work.

00:20:17.790 --> 00:20:20.270
And I carry that through
every step of the way.

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:23.358
That means when I have to make tough
decisions, I make tough decisions the way

00:20:23.358 --> 00:20:26.120
I would want someone to make
a tough decision with me.

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:29.659
It means when I have to communicate
difficult news, I communicate difficult

00:20:29.659 --> 00:20:32.940
news in a way I would want someone
to communicate that to me.

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:36.980
It means when I have to encourage
someone to change, or try something, or

00:20:36.980 --> 00:20:41.478
do something different, I make sure that
I am putting their interests first, and

00:20:41.478 --> 00:20:44.780
I'm helping them see what's in it for
them.

00:20:44.780 --> 00:20:47.755
So I think having that,
because I think when things get tough,

00:20:47.755 --> 00:20:51.880
it's easy to take your eyes off
the people and focus on the problem.

00:20:51.880 --> 00:20:55.719
And actually, I believe that's when you
need to lean into your people the most,

00:20:55.719 --> 00:20:58.212
because you can't solve
the problems by yourself.

00:20:58.212 --> 00:20:59.895
Winning is a team sport [LAUGH].

00:20:59.895 --> 00:21:04.357
And in order to win, it means you need all
the people on the team to believe in what

00:21:04.357 --> 00:21:08.237
you're saying to rally around you,
and to come together to do that.

00:21:08.237 --> 00:21:10.715
And so I firmly believe in
putting people first, and

00:21:10.715 --> 00:21:13.263
that's something that I
continue to do with my team.

00:21:13.263 --> 00:21:17.641
I'm really proud of this, because in my
entire career since I've started managing

00:21:17.641 --> 00:21:21.772
people, I have never ever had anybody
who directly reports to me and say, hey,

00:21:21.772 --> 00:21:23.580
here's my resignation notice.

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:28.111
It's never happened, because I always
have conversations with people.

00:21:28.111 --> 00:21:30.750
I always understand, hey,
how are things going?

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:33.810
Are you blooming where you're planted,
or is this a time for you to go to grow?

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:35.728
We have honest conversation.

00:21:35.728 --> 00:21:37.950
My team know they can rely on me.

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:40.300
My team know they can be honest with me.

00:21:40.300 --> 00:21:45.980
And so because of that,
because I put them first, guess what?

00:21:45.980 --> 00:21:47.557
They also put me first.

00:21:47.557 --> 00:21:50.121
They think about me, and
they think about the impact.

00:21:50.121 --> 00:21:53.180
And not only do they put, but
they put each other first.

00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:57.720
And so it allows us to navigate tough
times I think in a much better way.

00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:00.597
The second thing that I think is really
important is controlling what you

00:22:00.597 --> 00:22:01.760
can control.

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:04.700
There is so much you cannot control,
but guess what?

00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:06.315
There's so much you can.

00:22:06.315 --> 00:22:09.241
And how do you take
the assets that you have and

00:22:09.241 --> 00:22:12.480
do something with what you've got?

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:15.965
I think so many times we felt well,
if only this could change.

00:22:15.965 --> 00:22:16.828
You know what?

00:22:16.828 --> 00:22:18.210
Can't change that [LAUGH].

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:19.860
But there's a lot you can change.

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:21.709
So how about we do a lot with what we had?

00:22:21.709 --> 00:22:22.935
I'll give you an example.

00:22:22.935 --> 00:22:23.790
I'm a storyteller.

00:22:23.790 --> 00:22:25.110
I told you heard the beginning.

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:29.070
[LAUGH] One of the things that's
happened to us at Peloton is we have

00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:30.943
this passionate member base.

00:22:30.943 --> 00:22:32.354
I see some of them in the room.

00:22:32.354 --> 00:22:34.610
We're going to talk about your favorite
instructors after this [LAUGH].

00:22:34.610 --> 00:22:36.840
But we have a passionate member base.

00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:37.847
And what that means for

00:22:37.847 --> 00:22:41.033
us is as we've gone through
the different things we've gone through,

00:22:41.033 --> 00:22:44.232
our members have actually taken to
social media to tell their stories.

00:22:44.232 --> 00:22:48.096
So there was this phenomenon that was
happening, which was several members going

00:22:48.096 --> 00:22:51.660
to social media and saying, hey,
I was the biggest Peloton skeptic.

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:56.733
Here's what I actually wrote
publicly on social media in 2018.

00:22:56.733 --> 00:23:00.032
I will never, ever use Peloton.

00:23:00.032 --> 00:23:01.810
This is an overpriced coat rack.

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:03.280
Here's what I'm saying today.

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:05.492
This thing has changed my life, y'all.

00:23:05.492 --> 00:23:07.500
[LAUGH] This is the best
investment I've ever made.

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:10.230
They were publicly going to
social media to say this.

00:23:10.230 --> 00:23:11.090
And guess what?

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:12.790
Many times, they were tagging me.

00:23:12.790 --> 00:23:13.967
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And by the way,

00:23:13.967 --> 00:23:15.980
this was amazing therapy to
read at the end of the day.

00:23:15.980 --> 00:23:17.577
I was like, this is great.

00:23:17.577 --> 00:23:19.046
I'm not going to Twitter,
I'm going over here.

00:23:19.046 --> 00:23:20.810
This is amazing.

00:23:20.810 --> 00:23:25.754
And it was such a motivating experience,
because I was talking with my team,

00:23:25.754 --> 00:23:31.460
I had snotty, and I was like, what if
we just turned this into a campaign?

00:23:31.460 --> 00:23:34.109
And the whole team rallied,
it was amazing.

00:23:34.109 --> 00:23:35.780
It was the quickest campaign.

00:23:35.780 --> 00:23:37.427
Everyone was like, let us take this.

00:23:37.427 --> 00:23:38.620
And what is the campaign?

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:42.130
It's simple, it's literally
the person's name, what they said,

00:23:42.130 --> 00:23:46.340
what they said previously, and
what they're saying today, that's it.

00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:49.050
That's the ad, and we put it everywhere.

00:23:49.050 --> 00:23:50.184
And it was remarkable.

00:23:50.184 --> 00:23:53.880
Not did it galvanize our member base, but
it was inspiring for employees as well.

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:57.686
It was so great for people saying you know
what, there's always going to be skeptics,

00:23:57.686 --> 00:24:00.340
but there are also always
going to be believers.

00:24:00.340 --> 00:24:04.275
And it was such a magical thing,
and it meant so much to folks.

00:24:04.275 --> 00:24:08.967
And I think that's an example of I
couldn't control what everybody was

00:24:08.967 --> 00:24:13.750
saying, but I could shine a light on
something else that was being said.

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:15.744
I could shine a light on what
our members were saying.

00:24:15.744 --> 00:24:17.960
I could amplify the voices of our members.

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:20.330
So I think that's an example
of how you can look.

00:24:21.860 --> 00:24:24.418
Sometimes you gotta do a little
bit data mining, deep mining.

00:24:24.418 --> 00:24:26.791
But you can look at what you have,
what assets you have.

00:24:26.791 --> 00:24:28.933
You can use that to control the situation.

00:24:28.933 --> 00:24:32.968
And then third thing, final thing, I
won't judge GSB, keep it to three things-

00:24:32.968 --> 00:24:34.155
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:24:34.155 --> 00:24:36.325
&gt;&gt; Is communication, over communicate,

00:24:36.325 --> 00:24:37.930
I over communicate.

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:41.910
Every week with my team, I have weekly
standup with everybody in my organization.

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:43.730
It's my sacred time.

00:24:43.730 --> 00:24:46.755
The only time I miss it is if
there's something super important,

00:24:46.755 --> 00:24:49.900
urgent that I almost, my husband does,
he's sitting right here.

00:24:49.900 --> 00:24:53.040
Even when we're on vacation I'm like,
it's stand up time, y'all.

00:24:53.040 --> 00:24:54.252
And I'm getting in there,

00:24:54.252 --> 00:24:58.430
it's really important to me that I'm
connecting with my team every single week.

00:24:58.430 --> 00:25:00.851
Guess what?
During the hard weeks [LAUGH],

00:25:00.851 --> 00:25:01.755
I showed up.

00:25:01.755 --> 00:25:04.544
And I remember people on
my team said that to me.

00:25:04.544 --> 00:25:06.210
They were like,
we thought you were going to cancel?

00:25:07.240 --> 00:25:09.399
Because we were like, well,
is she going to show up today?

00:25:09.399 --> 00:25:11.210
Well, this going to be lit.

00:25:11.210 --> 00:25:12.305
I'm bringing my coffee to this.

00:25:12.305 --> 00:25:13.250
What's going on?

00:25:13.250 --> 00:25:15.130
But I show up.

00:25:15.130 --> 00:25:16.379
When it's hard, I show up.

00:25:16.379 --> 00:25:17.709
When it's easy, I show up.

00:25:17.709 --> 00:25:19.860
Holidays, I still show up.

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:22.270
And I think it's really important,
how do you show up?

00:25:22.270 --> 00:25:23.550
Are you always showing up?

00:25:23.550 --> 00:25:24.880
Are you communicating?

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:28.750
Are you sharing as much information
as you can with your team?

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:31.492
&gt;&gt; While you were at Stanford,
like many of us,

00:25:31.492 --> 00:25:35.976
you caught the entrepreneurial bug,
and you started two companies.

00:25:35.976 --> 00:25:39.104
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Did you want to speak about

00:25:39.104 --> 00:25:39.768
that?

00:25:39.768 --> 00:25:42.118
&gt;&gt; Well-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:25:42.118 --> 00:25:42.915
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Well,

00:25:42.915 --> 00:25:46.831
you then went on to build really
big global brands, Apple, GE.

00:25:46.831 --> 00:25:49.100
You were at Carbon, now you're at Peloton.

00:25:49.100 --> 00:25:54.000
Many of us are in the stages of deciding
whether or not we want to build or join.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:57.011
And I'd love to kind of know
a little bit more about your

00:25:57.011 --> 00:25:59.830
experience to making that decision for
yourself.

00:25:59.830 --> 00:26:03.199
Whether you would pursue what you were
building while you were at Stanford, or

00:26:03.199 --> 00:26:05.010
join another company.

00:26:05.010 --> 00:26:08.710
&gt;&gt; So the way I think about it,
I think about impact, right?

00:26:08.710 --> 00:26:10.480
You can have impact.

00:26:10.480 --> 00:26:13.380
You can have small impact, and
you can have really massive impact.

00:26:13.380 --> 00:26:17.125
And I remember when I first started,
when I was thinking I was going to be

00:26:17.125 --> 00:26:21.800
an entrepreneur, my husband said something
to me which was very interesting.

00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:26.496
He was like, I feel like you really
enjoy the management perspective more

00:26:26.496 --> 00:26:29.920
than the founder stuff,
going around fundraising.

00:26:29.920 --> 00:26:30.666
I was very nervous.

00:26:30.666 --> 00:26:32.850
I didn't want to take money from anybody.

00:26:32.850 --> 00:26:35.320
I just wanted us to continue
to pour our money into it.

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:36.815
That's not really how it works.

00:26:36.815 --> 00:26:41.930
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I love the management piece.

00:26:41.930 --> 00:26:43.671
I love the marketing of it.

00:26:43.671 --> 00:26:47.054
And so I started to have real
conversations with myself, because I think

00:26:47.054 --> 00:26:51.231
that's when at the GSB, during my class,
everybody was like, what are you doing?

00:26:51.231 --> 00:26:55.078
I'm starting my own tech startup, want to
come work for me and be my co-founder?

00:26:55.078 --> 00:26:56.833
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It was literally like,

00:26:56.833 --> 00:26:58.160
everyone was doing it.

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:00.355
So I didn't want to miss out.

00:27:00.355 --> 00:27:01.666
I was like, I don't want to miss out.

00:27:01.666 --> 00:27:04.000
I don't want to be the loser of my class.

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:05.965
Okay, I'm going to start something,
Cool Whip-

00:27:05.965 --> 00:27:06.838
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:27:06.838 --> 00:27:08.586
&gt;&gt; Shopping, something.

00:27:08.586 --> 00:27:09.602
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:27:09.602 --> 00:27:14.882
I think the lesson I learned there is
don't do what everyone else is doing.

00:27:14.882 --> 00:27:16.779
Do what's right for you.

00:27:16.779 --> 00:27:20.785
And for me, I remember it was very
actually humiliating when I first shut

00:27:20.785 --> 00:27:24.265
down all my startups [LAUGH],
because I would run into people and

00:27:24.265 --> 00:27:26.185
be like, how's my startup?

00:27:26.185 --> 00:27:27.410
Mine's still going, by the way.

00:27:27.410 --> 00:27:28.690
I hired my third engineer.

00:27:28.690 --> 00:27:30.950
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I'd be like,

00:27:30.950 --> 00:27:34.020
I shut down my startup and I got a job.

00:27:34.020 --> 00:27:36.830
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I was so humiliated.

00:27:36.830 --> 00:27:38.866
But guess what?

00:27:38.866 --> 00:27:41.119
I'm really happy about
the path that I took,

00:27:41.119 --> 00:27:43.148
because it was the path that was right for
me.

00:27:43.148 --> 00:27:47.326
And I'm sitting here because one of
my bosses and mentors, Joe DeSimone,

00:27:47.326 --> 00:27:48.200
is over there.

00:27:48.200 --> 00:27:50.690
Hi, Joe, show Joe some love, y'all.

00:27:50.690 --> 00:27:51.260
Joe's amazing.

00:27:51.260 --> 00:27:52.395
He's a professor here, too.

00:27:52.395 --> 00:27:56.128
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And I got to work for

00:27:56.128 --> 00:27:57.620
amazing people like Joe.

00:27:57.620 --> 00:28:01.642
Joe was the founder and CEO of Carbon,
and he hired me as his CMO.

00:28:01.642 --> 00:28:04.126
That was an amazing opportunity, right,

00:28:04.126 --> 00:28:07.095
to work in creating this
category of 3D printing.

00:28:07.095 --> 00:28:11.807
We have the largest use case of a 3D
printed consumer product out in the wild,

00:28:11.807 --> 00:28:14.400
with these Adidas 3D printed shoes.

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:16.010
And it was incredible to do that.

00:28:16.010 --> 00:28:20.996
And for me, what I've realized is,
I don't yet have an idea that I'm like,

00:28:20.996 --> 00:28:26.780
if I don't do this idea, I'm willing
to give everything up for this idea.

00:28:26.780 --> 00:28:29.737
So for me, it's like, well,
when I meet people like Joe,

00:28:29.737 --> 00:28:33.240
incredible people who have those ideas,
I want to contribute to that.

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:34.474
I want to help them build that.

00:28:34.474 --> 00:28:39.613
And I think for me,
it's about how can I have the most impact?

00:28:39.613 --> 00:28:44.541
And so far for me, it's about making
the most impact by being where

00:28:44.541 --> 00:28:47.369
I need to be at any given point in time.

00:28:47.369 --> 00:28:50.847
And for me, it's been joining these
incredible companies and helping scale and

00:28:50.847 --> 00:28:52.970
grow them as a marketing leader.

00:28:52.970 --> 00:28:55.810
So it's okay if you're not starting
a company, you'll be all right.

00:28:57.850 --> 00:29:02.738
&gt;&gt; On the side, you do invest in
people who are starting companies.

00:29:02.738 --> 00:29:08.003
And so from your perch now as a brand
expert, I'm curious to know what signs

00:29:08.003 --> 00:29:13.271
do you look for when you're evaluating
a company or a startup, and you want

00:29:13.271 --> 00:29:19.610
to declare them as having the right
mix of brand, product, strategy, etc?

00:29:19.610 --> 00:29:20.887
&gt;&gt; The passion of the founder.

00:29:20.887 --> 00:29:22.804
I just told you what I didn't have,
those people.

00:29:22.804 --> 00:29:24.007
I'm like, yep, those people.

00:29:24.007 --> 00:29:26.120
The people who I need to build this,

00:29:26.120 --> 00:29:29.180
I'm willing to give
everything up to build this.

00:29:29.180 --> 00:29:33.130
I believe in this so much, that I'm
willing to put everything on the line for

00:29:33.130 --> 00:29:35.660
this, that passion, because guess what?

00:29:35.660 --> 00:29:37.690
Your founder journey is not like this.

00:29:37.690 --> 00:29:38.957
That's not how it works.

00:29:38.957 --> 00:29:42.424
You might start out great with that
great press article that comes out, and

00:29:42.424 --> 00:29:44.830
then your consumers don't show up.

00:29:44.830 --> 00:29:46.820
And then you get product market fit.

00:29:46.820 --> 00:29:49.673
The unit economic model breaks down.

00:29:49.673 --> 00:29:51.461
But then [LAUGH], you know what?

00:29:51.461 --> 00:29:53.660
We got the unit economics to work.

00:29:53.660 --> 00:29:55.880
All scaling is really hard and
really hard.

00:29:55.880 --> 00:29:58.200
The journey is not linear.

00:29:58.200 --> 00:29:59.410
It's like a jungle gym.

00:29:59.410 --> 00:30:02.965
But if you have someone with passion,
I think that's really important.

00:30:02.965 --> 00:30:06.526
The second thing is, is this
someone that people want to follow?

00:30:06.526 --> 00:30:07.193
Do you know what I mean?

00:30:07.193 --> 00:30:09.532
You know that those people,
like Joe, people you love,

00:30:09.532 --> 00:30:10.700
you want to work for them?

00:30:10.700 --> 00:30:11.580
You're like [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:11.580 --> 00:30:14.750
When I'm excited,
my husband is sometimes a little too.

00:30:14.750 --> 00:30:17.220
When I'm passionate about something,
I'm working for it, right?

00:30:17.220 --> 00:30:17.995
I'm in it.

00:30:17.995 --> 00:30:20.985
And there are people who inspire you,
people who motivate.

00:30:20.985 --> 00:30:22.890
Not everyone has that gift.

00:30:22.890 --> 00:30:24.717
I think that's really important
to have as a founder.

00:30:24.717 --> 00:30:31.184
Because not only are you the idea
creator and generator, but guess what?

00:30:31.184 --> 00:30:33.950
You're the first CMO, and
you're the first head of sales.

00:30:33.950 --> 00:30:37.170
You need to recruit and
attract people to that idea.

00:30:37.170 --> 00:30:41.080
So having that magnetism, being someone
that people are willing to like, yeah,

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:41.825
you know what?

00:30:41.825 --> 00:30:44.860
I'm going to take a risk, and
come join me on this adventure.

00:30:44.860 --> 00:30:46.360
I think that's really important.

00:30:46.360 --> 00:30:49.230
And then the third thing is curiosity.

00:30:50.230 --> 00:30:53.815
When I'm looking to invest and
I ask questions, and

00:30:53.815 --> 00:30:58.720
the person has an answer to everything,
I'm not going to invest.

00:30:58.720 --> 00:31:01.468
Because the reality is,
there are many things we don't know, and

00:31:01.468 --> 00:31:03.950
we need to be comfortable saying,
you know what?

00:31:03.950 --> 00:31:05.730
I don't actually know that.

00:31:05.730 --> 00:31:07.076
But I'm going to go and
I'm going to learn and

00:31:07.076 --> 00:31:09.190
I'm going to get back to you about that.

00:31:09.190 --> 00:31:10.774
And guess what?
If they actually get back to me,

00:31:10.774 --> 00:31:11.862
they get extra bonus points.

00:31:11.862 --> 00:31:13.171
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because many people say that they

00:31:13.171 --> 00:31:13.754
don't follow up.

00:31:13.754 --> 00:31:16.502
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But it's that curiosity,

00:31:16.502 --> 00:31:18.600
not thinking you know everything.

00:31:18.600 --> 00:31:19.880
None of us know everything.

00:31:19.880 --> 00:31:22.174
I feel like we live in
the age of backseat drivers.

00:31:22.174 --> 00:31:26.790
My god, I read this tweet,
one tweet, and I know everything.

00:31:26.790 --> 00:31:29.675
Backseat drivers will drive
you off a cliff, believe that.

00:31:29.675 --> 00:31:32.127
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Backseat drivers who drive you

00:31:32.127 --> 00:31:33.010
off a cliff.

00:31:33.010 --> 00:31:36.784
So I think it's really important that
people are curious, they're willing to

00:31:36.784 --> 00:31:40.390
ask questions, and they're willing
to admit what they don't know.

00:31:40.390 --> 00:31:44.364
And it's okay, because I think people who
are humble enough to say you know what,

00:31:44.364 --> 00:31:46.950
I don't know everything,
I don't know that.

00:31:46.950 --> 00:31:50.706
Those people always are more successful
than people who think they have all

00:31:50.706 --> 00:31:52.220
the answers.

00:31:52.220 --> 00:31:54.411
&gt;&gt; I want to switch gears and
talk about identity,

00:31:54.411 --> 00:31:56.208
because I know that racial justice and

00:31:56.208 --> 00:32:00.860
gender equity are issues that you are
incredibly passionate and supportive of.

00:32:00.860 --> 00:32:03.047
You are so interesting to me,

00:32:03.047 --> 00:32:08.530
because you had to learn what it
means to be black, in America.

00:32:08.530 --> 00:32:12.617
Many people might believe that being black
or black identity is a monolith, but

00:32:12.617 --> 00:32:15.423
in Nigeria, where you were born and
where you grew up,

00:32:15.423 --> 00:32:17.500
doesn't really exist as an ethnicity.

00:32:17.500 --> 00:32:21.160
There are 500 other ethnicities,
and black doesn't show up there.

00:32:21.160 --> 00:32:25.664
In London or in the United Kingdom, where
you went to school and then in the US,

00:32:25.664 --> 00:32:29.848
that's really where like this racial
construct started taking shape.

00:32:29.848 --> 00:32:34.097
I'd love for you to share with us what
that journey was like for you and

00:32:34.097 --> 00:32:37.320
what were some of the lessons
that you learned as your

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:39.897
identity mutated across these borders.

00:32:39.897 --> 00:32:46.310
&gt;&gt; It's so funny, because growing up
in Nigeria, Yes everybody was black,

00:32:46.310 --> 00:32:50.810
but there was colorism, which I like
to think of as a shade of racism.

00:32:50.810 --> 00:32:56.247
And growing up, I wasn't light skinned,
but I wasn't in Nigeria,

00:32:56.247 --> 00:33:01.964
I wasn't dark skinned, I was in the middle
and my older sister is dark and

00:33:01.964 --> 00:33:07.963
almost everybody I met, we met will
always say, Tara you're so light and so

00:33:07.963 --> 00:33:14.490
pretty and that and they would miss they
would look at my sister and they would.

00:33:14.490 --> 00:33:21.310
And I remember growing up just
hearing that over and over again.

00:33:21.310 --> 00:33:26.274
And Nigeria is a former colony, right and
it's amazing what that does to you and

00:33:26.274 --> 00:33:28.148
what that can do to your mind.

00:33:28.148 --> 00:33:31.560
And, I should have never
told this story before.

00:33:31.560 --> 00:33:34.140
I don't even know why I'm sharing this,
maybe I'm all emotional in the GSB.

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:39.171
But, I remember growing up,
there was a time where I was,

00:33:39.171 --> 00:33:41.900
I felt I wanted to make my skin lighter.

00:33:41.900 --> 00:33:44.746
I went to the store and
didn't have a lot of money, so

00:33:44.746 --> 00:33:47.220
I bought the worst possible cream.

00:33:47.220 --> 00:33:48.230
I put it on my skin.

00:33:48.230 --> 00:33:49.310
It was awful.

00:33:49.310 --> 00:33:51.210
I had a complete allergic reaction.

00:33:51.210 --> 00:33:56.530
I literally transformed my skin into,
I don't know, I looked like a leopard.

00:33:56.530 --> 00:33:57.730
It was not good.

00:33:57.730 --> 00:34:02.130
And I'm glad that actually happened,
because I was like, you know what?

00:34:02.130 --> 00:34:06.550
Better to be one color and look great,
so not ever going to do that again.

00:34:06.550 --> 00:34:11.013
And, but, I learned a painful lesson,
when I was very young, but

00:34:11.013 --> 00:34:13.260
I was glad I learned that lesson.

00:34:14.620 --> 00:34:17.411
But when I came to the US,
it was interesting,

00:34:17.411 --> 00:34:22.650
because I remember someone referred to me,
the dark skinned girl and I was like, who?

00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:25.630
And it was like, you're talking about me?

00:34:25.630 --> 00:34:30.157
I remember that was actually my very
first vivid memory, where I was, wow,

00:34:30.157 --> 00:34:34.170
I'm now the one,
I'm now the dark skinned person.

00:34:34.170 --> 00:34:39.246
And then, I also just, all
the experiences I had of walking through

00:34:39.246 --> 00:34:45.310
sites like Harvard Yard and, people
wondering if I went to school there.

00:34:45.310 --> 00:34:50.346
And those experiences, or people thinking
I was the cleaner, or I actually

00:34:50.346 --> 00:34:55.320
had to do dorm crew, which is if you
went to Harvard, you know what that is?

00:34:55.320 --> 00:34:58.728
It's where you clean
the dormitories of kids, and

00:34:58.728 --> 00:35:02.296
many times it's
the underrepresented minorities,

00:35:02.296 --> 00:35:07.528
cleaning the dorms of our rich white
counterparts, and that can be painful and

00:35:07.528 --> 00:35:11.809
it can be and I remember just going
through these experiences and

00:35:11.809 --> 00:35:16.520
just realizing when I walk out the door,
nobody cares where I am.

00:35:16.520 --> 00:35:17.730
I'm just black.

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:19.561
I'm a black woman in America.

00:35:19.561 --> 00:35:21.869
What am I going to do about that?

00:35:21.869 --> 00:35:25.520
And I remember asking myself,
what am I going to do about that?

00:35:25.520 --> 00:35:27.937
At the time, I didn't really have much.

00:35:27.937 --> 00:35:28.990
[LAUGH] I didn't.

00:35:28.990 --> 00:35:33.270
But I thought to myself, well, how about
I try to do the best with what I have?

00:35:33.270 --> 00:35:34.810
I'm going to a great school.

00:35:34.810 --> 00:35:38.145
How about I try to be the best
leader that I can be and

00:35:38.145 --> 00:35:42.580
try to model what it's like to
excel as a black woman in business.

00:35:42.580 --> 00:35:47.355
That's what I'm interested in and
that's what I've tried to do, and that's,

00:35:47.355 --> 00:35:49.200
that's my passion.

00:35:49.200 --> 00:35:55.310
That's why I show up every
day no matter how hard it is.

00:35:55.310 --> 00:35:59.205
For many people, I'm the first
black female boss that they have.

00:35:59.205 --> 00:36:04.050
And I think about that, what do I want?

00:36:04.050 --> 00:36:06.830
I think, well,
some people are going to walk in here, and

00:36:06.830 --> 00:36:08.970
they're going to not have any assumptions.

00:36:08.970 --> 00:36:11.599
Some people are going to walk
up with some assumptions.

00:36:11.599 --> 00:36:13.470
[LAUGH] What can I do to change that?

00:36:14.580 --> 00:36:16.240
What can I do to impact that?

00:36:16.240 --> 00:36:17.250
I think about that a lot.

00:36:17.250 --> 00:36:22.794
And so for me, my journey has been about,
how I can help elevate black women,

00:36:22.794 --> 00:36:26.974
how and women of color in general
in the United States, and

00:36:26.974 --> 00:36:31.040
how I can use my platform to show and
demystify.

00:36:31.040 --> 00:36:32.630
And many people say, you know what?

00:36:32.630 --> 00:36:35.402
If you're a young black girl,
you know what, it darkens or

00:36:35.402 --> 00:36:36.737
you could definitely do it.

00:36:36.737 --> 00:36:39.511
[LAUGH] I just really try to
inspire people and show people,

00:36:39.511 --> 00:36:41.560
because you can't be what you can't see.

00:36:41.560 --> 00:36:43.940
And I think it's important to see that.

00:36:43.940 --> 00:36:48.068
It was really important for
me to see Ursula Burns,

00:36:48.068 --> 00:36:52.750
first black woman,
CEO of a Fortune 500 company.

00:36:52.750 --> 00:36:54.030
That meant so much to me.

00:36:54.030 --> 00:36:55.300
I've never even met her.

00:36:55.300 --> 00:36:56.910
I talk about her all the time.

00:36:56.910 --> 00:36:57.890
People think we're friends.

00:36:57.890 --> 00:36:59.580
No, I've never met her, love you.

00:36:59.580 --> 00:37:02.134
You invite her from the top, let me know.

00:37:02.134 --> 00:37:04.960
[LAUGH] Yeah, but you meant so much to me.

00:37:04.960 --> 00:37:10.695
And now here we are in 2022,
there's still only three,

00:37:10.695 --> 00:37:15.960
Ursula Burns, Ross Breuer,
Cassandra Docket.

00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:17.000
That's it.

00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:19.560
It's 2022.
What are we going to do about that?

00:37:19.560 --> 00:37:21.730
I ask myself that question.

00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:27.714
&gt;&gt; That intersection that you
described of race and gender,

00:37:27.714 --> 00:37:32.318
you spoke to a little bit and
your response, is so nuanced and

00:37:32.318 --> 00:37:36.651
I think, I sit in the same place and
especially in the US,

00:37:36.651 --> 00:37:40.892
it comes with so much baggage,
and so what did you have to

00:37:40.892 --> 00:37:45.710
unlearn about that identity in order for
you to be successful?

00:37:47.090 --> 00:37:52.375
&gt;&gt; When I first started out,
I don't want to talk about anything black,

00:37:52.375 --> 00:37:57.840
I don't want people to see me as
the trope of the angry black woman.

00:37:57.840 --> 00:38:01.981
&gt;&gt; So like I want to just be
easy peasy Dara, calm, neutral,

00:38:01.981 --> 00:38:06.550
okay to be around,
never gets annoyed about anything.

00:38:06.550 --> 00:38:08.782
And I wasn't really showing up as myself,

00:38:08.782 --> 00:38:13.390
because I'm sure you've already seen
I'm pretty expressive, passionate.

00:38:13.390 --> 00:38:16.224
And so I was just like,
don't say anything,

00:38:16.224 --> 00:38:18.697
don't do anything, don't be weird.

00:38:18.697 --> 00:38:21.740
And I just was like a shadow of myself.

00:38:21.740 --> 00:38:28.740
And I remember this incredible boss
that I had and he was amazing.

00:38:28.740 --> 00:38:32.238
And he is gay and and he's other and
one day he pulled me aside and

00:38:32.238 --> 00:38:36.210
he was like we other people come,
let's have a little talk.

00:38:36.210 --> 00:38:38.470
And he said you could be yourself.

00:38:38.470 --> 00:38:40.140
I'll let you know if you get to fun.

00:38:40.140 --> 00:38:41.200
How about that?

00:38:41.200 --> 00:38:45.688
And that was amazing to me, right, to have
someone that I knew was looking out for

00:38:45.688 --> 00:38:48.262
me and
he understood what I was going through,

00:38:48.262 --> 00:38:51.230
how I was trying to navigate these spaces.

00:38:51.230 --> 00:38:54.190
And that's why I think mentorship is so
important.

00:38:54.190 --> 00:38:58.910
And it wasn't the CEO of
Goldman Sachs that was mentoring me.

00:38:58.910 --> 00:39:01.425
I feel like a lot of times I
get notes from people, hey,

00:39:01.425 --> 00:39:03.250
I would like you to be my mentor.

00:39:03.250 --> 00:39:07.143
And sometimes I reply, and I'm like, have
you asked any other black women in your

00:39:07.143 --> 00:39:10.720
organization or anybody you know
that has shown an interest in you.

00:39:10.720 --> 00:39:15.820
I think sometimes we think we need the top
of the leaderboard, to be our mentor.

00:39:15.820 --> 00:39:20.074
And sometimes you don't, that's, it's
actually who cares about you the most,

00:39:20.074 --> 00:39:23.415
just that's who you should be your mentor,
who believes in you,

00:39:23.415 --> 00:39:25.510
who wants to invest time in you.

00:39:25.510 --> 00:39:27.672
And I learned so much from him,

00:39:27.672 --> 00:39:32.160
it helped me realize that I
could show up more as myself.

00:39:32.160 --> 00:39:35.640
Guess what,
I also realized there's a cost to that.

00:39:35.640 --> 00:39:38.205
When you're the person
that makes the comments,

00:39:38.205 --> 00:39:41.590
wouldn't it be great if
we have a diverse slate?

00:39:41.590 --> 00:39:43.920
There's some people who
are not going to like that.

00:39:43.920 --> 00:39:45.530
It's going to upset some people.

00:39:45.530 --> 00:39:50.322
When you make comments about, hey,
can we make sure that we're promoting,

00:39:50.322 --> 00:39:54.320
because I think about recruit,
retain, promote and protect.

00:39:55.470 --> 00:39:58.380
Cause guess what,
everybody says they want diversity.

00:39:58.380 --> 00:40:02.460
When you add a diverse person to
an organization, guess what happens?

00:40:02.460 --> 00:40:07.560
If it's like a transplant, you add
an organ that the body wants to reject.

00:40:08.970 --> 00:40:12.523
It's just the truth,
because it's a lot easier,

00:40:12.523 --> 00:40:15.320
it's easier when we're all the same.

00:40:15.320 --> 00:40:17.220
It's easier when we all
think the same thing.

00:40:17.220 --> 00:40:20.160
It's harder when we have different
perspectives and points of view.

00:40:20.160 --> 00:40:22.061
It takes work.

00:40:22.061 --> 00:40:23.190
Are we willing to do the work?

00:40:23.190 --> 00:40:25.621
We should all be willing to do the work.

00:40:25.621 --> 00:40:29.509
And I think what I've done is,
well, what I try to do every day is

00:40:29.509 --> 00:40:34.189
make sure that I'm that voice in the room,
that I ask those tough questions,

00:40:34.189 --> 00:40:37.650
that I push those issues,
doesn't come without a cost.

00:40:37.650 --> 00:40:42.323
But what I've also learned is
that there's a time and a place.

00:40:42.323 --> 00:40:43.847
And as a black woman,

00:40:43.847 --> 00:40:49.457
what I've realized is many times people
want to know are you really on the team?

00:40:51.820 --> 00:40:52.760
Say that again.

00:40:52.760 --> 00:40:54.210
People want to know if you're on the team.

00:40:54.210 --> 00:40:55.580
What do I mean by that?

00:40:55.580 --> 00:40:58.520
People want to understand,
why are you making that comment?

00:40:58.520 --> 00:41:01.625
Are you making that comment
just to be disruptive, or

00:41:01.625 --> 00:41:05.150
are you making that comment
because you want us to be better?

00:41:05.150 --> 00:41:10.190
So what I try to do is make sure do
everything that I can to make sure people

00:41:10.190 --> 00:41:14.819
understand I'm on the team, but
I want us as a team to get better.

00:41:14.819 --> 00:41:16.350
And that's how I try to navigate.

00:41:16.350 --> 00:41:20.000
It's not easy, and I don't do it well all
the time, but that's how I try to show up.

00:41:21.060 --> 00:41:25.042
&gt;&gt; As one of the few black execs at
Peloton and also in the tech industry,

00:41:25.042 --> 00:41:29.781
just more broadly, I'm certain that you
get pulled into a lot of conversations,

00:41:29.781 --> 00:41:33.763
where it might not really fall within
your scope as the marketer, but

00:41:33.763 --> 00:41:36.310
it's so core to your being.

00:41:36.310 --> 00:41:40.691
I'm curious about how you manage
the weight of that responsibility of being

00:41:40.691 --> 00:41:42.950
a steward on behalf of your community,

00:41:42.950 --> 00:41:46.850
whether that's black women,
immigrants, etc.

00:41:46.850 --> 00:41:53.589
And what do you believe that others
allies could be doing better to help so

00:41:53.589 --> 00:41:58.130
that you don't have to shoulder so
much of that?

00:42:01.430 --> 00:42:05.933
&gt;&gt; Well, there's a saying,
it's not my thing, but I love the thing.

00:42:05.933 --> 00:42:07.389
Pressure is a privilege.

00:42:09.358 --> 00:42:11.770
Pressure is a privilege,
I think about that.

00:42:11.770 --> 00:42:17.000
For the hard days and during those hard
days, I remember I'm here for a reason.

00:42:19.190 --> 00:42:21.730
I literally told myself, Dora,
pressure is a privilege.

00:42:23.700 --> 00:42:25.873
And when you are in
a position of privilege,

00:42:25.873 --> 00:42:28.160
what are you going to do about it?

00:42:28.160 --> 00:42:31.626
So I say, if I think this is hard,
how much harder is it for

00:42:31.626 --> 00:42:34.460
people who aren't yet
where I am in my career?

00:42:36.340 --> 00:42:38.144
So I can't be like, this is too hard.

00:42:38.144 --> 00:42:42.997
You know what, I told myself,
grow up, life's hard.

00:42:42.997 --> 00:42:46.945
Keep pushing, because I have
a daughter who's six years old,

00:42:46.945 --> 00:42:51.160
I want my daughter to be dealing
with different things, right?

00:42:51.160 --> 00:42:52.840
I don't want my daughter
to be dealing with this.

00:42:52.840 --> 00:42:56.940
I don't want my daughter to be
having these same conversations.

00:42:56.940 --> 00:42:59.720
So that means I have to be
willing to do the work.

00:43:00.870 --> 00:43:03.359
And I'm willing to do the work,
and I do the work.

00:43:03.359 --> 00:43:05.460
Doesn't mean it doesn't get tough.

00:43:05.460 --> 00:43:07.820
That's why I think you
need a support system.

00:43:07.820 --> 00:43:12.650
I have my amazing husband who's here and
my sister, and my family, and my friends.

00:43:12.650 --> 00:43:15.422
And my mentors Rob Segal,
I don't know if you're here.

00:43:15.422 --> 00:43:17.050
Rob, yeah, I have a good Rob story.

00:43:17.050 --> 00:43:18.026
We're going to come back to that.

00:43:18.026 --> 00:43:19.070
I'm going to tell you that one,
it's a good one.

00:43:20.210 --> 00:43:23.665
And I have these people that
support me and love me, right?

00:43:23.665 --> 00:43:25.970
And so I have a support system.

00:43:25.970 --> 00:43:29.508
That's really important,
because you know what, it gets hard.

00:43:29.508 --> 00:43:32.406
And some days I'm going to go home and I'm
going to cry, and I'm going to be like,

00:43:32.406 --> 00:43:34.320
this is really, really hard.

00:43:34.320 --> 00:43:38.030
And I need someone,
a people to remind me of who I am and

00:43:38.030 --> 00:43:40.640
why I'm doing what I'm doing.

00:43:40.640 --> 00:43:43.430
So I think that support system is so key.

00:43:43.430 --> 00:43:45.710
And I think that's
something that the GSB and

00:43:45.710 --> 00:43:48.290
the Stanford community really provides,
right?

00:43:48.290 --> 00:43:50.430
My very best friend in the world,
I met at the GSB.

00:43:51.690 --> 00:43:57.410
And my husband and I, we have a Zoom call
with her and her husband every Sunday.

00:43:58.500 --> 00:44:02.730
And she's my sister, my support system,
we talk about everything.

00:44:02.730 --> 00:44:07.080
So I think having a support system is so
key and so important to navigating that.

00:44:07.080 --> 00:44:10.710
And then the third thing I'll
say is protect your peace.

00:44:10.710 --> 00:44:12.609
You have to protect your peace.

00:44:14.799 --> 00:44:18.567
I remember the very first time
a press article was written about me,

00:44:18.567 --> 00:44:19.436
I was so proud.

00:44:19.436 --> 00:44:22.298
I was so excited, I emailed it to my mom.

00:44:22.298 --> 00:44:26.182
My mom was like, really great, Dora,

00:44:26.182 --> 00:44:30.429
you're never as great as they say you are.

00:44:30.429 --> 00:44:31.957
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That sounds like a Nigerian mother.

00:44:31.957 --> 00:44:34.452
&gt;&gt; I know, it sounds like
a Nigeria mother, yeah, exactly.

00:44:34.452 --> 00:44:38.857
All the emphasis, you are never
as great as they say you are.

00:44:38.857 --> 00:44:42.070
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's where the humility comes from.

00:44:42.070 --> 00:44:44.770
&gt;&gt; But you're also never as
terrible as they say you are.

00:44:46.040 --> 00:44:47.840
God is still doing work with you.

00:44:47.840 --> 00:44:50.200
Literally what she said to me, verbatim.

00:44:50.200 --> 00:44:52.949
I remember it,
I think about that a lot, right?

00:44:52.949 --> 00:44:56.000
And I think that's really important,
because when you're doing the work that

00:44:56.000 --> 00:44:58.440
you're going through,
you get criticized, right?

00:44:58.440 --> 00:44:59.470
Are you doing enough?

00:44:59.470 --> 00:45:00.760
Are you doing too much?

00:45:00.760 --> 00:45:04.487
For some people, you're never
doing enough, for some people,

00:45:04.487 --> 00:45:06.800
you're always doing too much, right?

00:45:06.800 --> 00:45:11.588
And then there are people who are like,
yay, thank God for those people, yay,

00:45:11.588 --> 00:45:12.101
right?

00:45:12.101 --> 00:45:13.521
And you got to navigate all of that.

00:45:13.521 --> 00:45:15.940
And so for me,
it's about protecting my peace and

00:45:15.940 --> 00:45:19.750
making sure that I don't allow those
backseat drivers to drive me off a cliff.

00:45:20.790 --> 00:45:22.710
&gt;&gt; My final question for you.

00:45:22.710 --> 00:45:25.590
So you alluded to this earlier,
there's the infamous question

00:45:25.590 --> 00:45:28.850
on the GSB application,
what matters to you most and why?

00:45:28.850 --> 00:45:31.869
How did you answer this ten years
ago when you were applying?

00:45:31.869 --> 00:45:32.750
And how would you answer this now?

00:45:34.770 --> 00:45:36.060
&gt;&gt; That's a really good question.

00:45:36.060 --> 00:45:37.122
I'm going to answer it.

00:45:37.122 --> 00:45:40.870
First, I'm going to tell you my Rob Segal
story because I think it's really

00:45:40.870 --> 00:45:41.593
important.

00:45:41.593 --> 00:45:43.256
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; He's sweating right now.

00:45:43.256 --> 00:45:44.846
&gt;&gt; He's like why,
why is this happening right now?

00:45:44.846 --> 00:45:46.831
No, I think it's really important.

00:45:46.831 --> 00:45:47.650
It's really important.

00:45:47.650 --> 00:45:49.060
There's a lesson here.

00:45:49.060 --> 00:45:54.940
Before my first CMO job, this is the big
deal, my first ever C-Suite job.

00:45:54.940 --> 00:45:56.981
I was interviewing for it.

00:45:56.981 --> 00:45:58.840
And I was like, I need someone to talk to.

00:45:58.840 --> 00:46:00.620
Rob, do you remember this?

00:46:00.620 --> 00:46:04.760
And I'm like, wow, SOS,
could we have a conversation?

00:46:04.760 --> 00:46:07.224
He was on a plane flying somewhere.

00:46:07.224 --> 00:46:08.812
He was like, what time is it?

00:46:08.812 --> 00:46:10.870
I'm going to get on phone,
I'm going to call you.

00:46:10.870 --> 00:46:13.600
He called me, had a conversation,
gave me that confidence.

00:46:13.600 --> 00:46:17.780
I say that because it's so important
to be there for each other, right?

00:46:17.780 --> 00:46:20.002
And that was so critical for me.

00:46:20.002 --> 00:46:23.036
At the time I had imposter syndrome,
I was definitely ready for the job.

00:46:23.036 --> 00:46:23.773
But guess what?

00:46:23.773 --> 00:46:26.110
I'd never done it before.

00:46:26.110 --> 00:46:29.870
I like to say, there's a lot of half
baked people walking around looking for

00:46:29.870 --> 00:46:32.068
fully baked people, some of those people.

00:46:32.068 --> 00:46:34.823
There's a first time before you do,
all of us have a first time.

00:46:34.823 --> 00:46:38.817
Now I've been a CMO multiple times, but
there was the first time someone was

00:46:38.817 --> 00:46:41.580
going to give me that shot,
give me that opportunity.

00:46:41.580 --> 00:46:46.247
Sue Segal, incredible woman who gave
me that first shot, amazing leader.

00:46:46.247 --> 00:46:50.040
I needed the confidence to
know that I could do that.

00:46:50.040 --> 00:46:51.710
Rob gave me that confidence.

00:46:51.710 --> 00:46:52.460
He sat with me.

00:46:52.460 --> 00:46:53.600
He listened to me.

00:46:53.600 --> 00:46:55.420
He helped me prep.

00:46:55.420 --> 00:46:57.360
Invaluable, changed my life.

00:46:58.750 --> 00:47:00.710
You never know when you're going to
change someone else's life.

00:47:00.710 --> 00:47:04.487
So when someone in the Stanford
community reaches out to you, SOS,

00:47:04.487 --> 00:47:06.180
don't ever be too busy.

00:47:06.180 --> 00:47:10.124
I think about that now, by the way, when
people reach out to me, I remember Rob,

00:47:10.124 --> 00:47:12.690
and I remember hey, I'm not too important.

00:47:12.690 --> 00:47:15.844
I'm not too busy to be there for
my friends and

00:47:15.844 --> 00:47:18.753
be there for the people that matter to me.

00:47:18.753 --> 00:47:19.698
Okay, now I'll answer your question.

00:47:19.698 --> 00:47:21.383
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Thank you.

00:47:21.383 --> 00:47:24.770
&gt;&gt; So, what matters most to me and why?

00:47:24.770 --> 00:47:28.646
I wrote what matters most, and
Derek Moulton, thank you for

00:47:28.646 --> 00:47:31.772
accepting me to the GSB,
[LAUGH] he's over here.

00:47:31.772 --> 00:47:36.170
And I wrote, what matters most
to me is my Christian faith.

00:47:36.170 --> 00:47:40.237
And it's because it gives me
the strength to live by my values,

00:47:40.237 --> 00:47:44.915
was what mattered most to me then,
it is what matters to me most to me now,

00:47:44.915 --> 00:47:47.564
it is what will always matter most to me.

00:47:47.564 --> 00:47:52.303
My values of love, of acceptance,
of respect, of inclusiveness.

00:47:52.303 --> 00:47:56.943
I talked earlier about treating people
how I want to be treated, there's so

00:47:56.943 --> 00:47:59.236
much in the world we can't control.

00:47:59.236 --> 00:48:03.370
But I think many times sometimes
we forget about forgiveness.

00:48:03.370 --> 00:48:07.300
If we forget about love,
we forget about respect.

00:48:09.300 --> 00:48:14.171
And it's always about what my way,
my view, my perspective, my thought.

00:48:14.171 --> 00:48:19.259
And for me, my faith reminds me
not just to think about myself,

00:48:19.259 --> 00:48:22.919
but more importantly
to think about others.

00:48:24.940 --> 00:48:30.370
And as a leader my faith inspires
me to be a servant leader.

00:48:30.370 --> 00:48:34.570
I tell my team I want to serve you,
I'm here to serve you.

00:48:34.570 --> 00:48:37.390
And for me that's what matters most.

00:48:37.390 --> 00:48:41.341
And at the end of the day when I
talked about that protection of peace,

00:48:41.341 --> 00:48:42.159
[LAUGH] Right,

00:48:42.159 --> 00:48:46.743
we're all going to go out in the world I'm
sure I sit here all this amazing people.

00:48:46.743 --> 00:48:51.148
Maybe you'll hire me and
you going to build great things and

00:48:51.148 --> 00:48:53.637
you're going to do great things.

00:48:53.637 --> 00:48:56.717
And I think it's really important not
to forget, for me it's my faith, for

00:48:56.717 --> 00:48:58.090
you be something else, right?

00:48:58.090 --> 00:49:01.509
One of my friends is an atheist
like I don't get this whole thing,

00:49:01.509 --> 00:49:02.825
I'm like that's okay.

00:49:02.825 --> 00:49:04.870
For you, it's going to be something else.

00:49:04.870 --> 00:49:08.200
But whatever matters most to you and
why don't ever forget it, and

00:49:08.200 --> 00:49:09.460
don't lose sight of it.

00:49:10.670 --> 00:49:13.190
&gt;&gt; Thank you Dora you
are truly remarkable.

00:49:13.190 --> 00:49:15.513
And I knew that when you interviewed me.

00:49:15.513 --> 00:49:19.130
[LAUGH] We're going to turn
to some audience question.

00:49:19.130 --> 00:49:19.850
&gt;&gt; Hi, Dora.

00:49:19.850 --> 00:49:21.660
First of all, thank you for being here.

00:49:21.660 --> 00:49:22.530
My name is Melanie.

00:49:22.530 --> 00:49:24.310
I'm an MBA too.

00:49:24.310 --> 00:49:28.761
I also wanted to say a big thank you to
your family I actually went to life when I

00:49:28.761 --> 00:49:29.886
was 6 years old.

00:49:29.886 --> 00:49:30.420
&gt;&gt; My God.
[LAUGH]

00:49:30.420 --> 00:49:33.349
&gt;&gt; That was a really big transition for

00:49:33.349 --> 00:49:39.106
me when I was moving from London, and
I used to cry a lot at the school and

00:49:39.106 --> 00:49:43.222
your mom used to make pancakes for
me in the house.

00:49:43.222 --> 00:49:44.589
[LAUGH] So thank you so much.

00:49:44.589 --> 00:49:45.720
They're all my favorite.

00:49:46.950 --> 00:49:50.570
But my question is one about family life.

00:49:50.570 --> 00:49:54.880
And I know that men don't often get this
on this stage, but it's so important.

00:49:54.880 --> 00:49:56.480
Yeah, it's so important for us.

00:49:56.480 --> 00:49:59.835
As black women in the school we often
talk about that there aren't enough

00:49:59.835 --> 00:50:03.820
leaders in front of us that can talk
to us about our lives outside of work.

00:50:03.820 --> 00:50:08.921
And so I wanted to talk to you about,
how you balance everything but

00:50:08.921 --> 00:50:14.931
not just that how you are very intentional
about, the qualities in your home,

00:50:14.931 --> 00:50:20.512
looking after your kids, raising that
family in the US as an immigrant.

00:50:20.512 --> 00:50:24.152
There's so many questions in this but it's
really just about balance and family and

00:50:24.152 --> 00:50:25.340
the choices that you made.

00:50:27.040 --> 00:50:32.015
&gt;&gt; I'm also interested in
the answer to this question.

00:50:32.015 --> 00:50:32.940
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That's funny.

00:50:32.940 --> 00:50:35.410
That's my husband, he likes jokes.

00:50:35.410 --> 00:50:41.230
So, look,
I'm going to keep it real, right?

00:50:42.330 --> 00:50:45.740
There is no balance in my life,
there is only harmony.

00:50:45.740 --> 00:50:49.420
Many times, I'm on a balance sometimes.

00:50:49.420 --> 00:50:50.720
It's been a year for me.

00:50:50.720 --> 00:50:53.960
I don't know if the year
I bet it's been a year.

00:50:53.960 --> 00:50:55.200
And you know what?

00:50:55.200 --> 00:50:58.067
Work has come first a lot of the year,
and I'm so

00:50:58.067 --> 00:51:01.960
blessed that I have an incredible
husband who also has a career.

00:51:01.960 --> 00:51:05.591
So we're dual career,
couple of dual career family, but

00:51:05.591 --> 00:51:07.750
it's not my job to raise the kids.

00:51:07.750 --> 00:51:08.340
It's our job.

00:51:09.400 --> 00:51:12.200
We're thinking about that and
we're looking at that.

00:51:12.200 --> 00:51:15.783
And guess what, sometimes when things
are really crazy for me, he's like, okay,

00:51:15.783 --> 00:51:17.010
I'm lead parent now.

00:51:17.010 --> 00:51:17.530
Okay?

00:51:17.530 --> 00:51:21.079
So you're not just going to breathe in
here with your 30 seconds of thoughts and

00:51:21.079 --> 00:51:21.778
breathe out and

00:51:21.778 --> 00:51:24.970
make the pancakes so I want to make
the pancakes you just said pancakes.

00:51:24.970 --> 00:51:26.170
So that's in my head.

00:51:26.170 --> 00:51:29.950
And I think that's really
important that partnership.

00:51:29.950 --> 00:51:32.170
It is so key and so important.

00:51:32.170 --> 00:51:35.811
But the other thing too is
I tried to do with my kids,

00:51:35.811 --> 00:51:39.382
I have a daughter who's six and
a son who's four.

00:51:39.382 --> 00:51:42.286
And my daughter who's six, she kind of
understands things a little bit more now,

00:51:42.286 --> 00:51:44.950
as I have conversations with her,
Hey, what's really important to you?

00:51:44.950 --> 00:51:48.920
She's got a musical
coming up on ME in May.

00:51:48.920 --> 00:51:52.760
It's very important to her,
it's in person.

00:51:53.780 --> 00:51:56.300
You can join on zoom but it's in person.

00:51:57.320 --> 00:52:03.915
As she said to me, mommy, this is in
person and I want you there in person.

00:52:03.915 --> 00:52:07.911
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You don't know my daughter Zoey,

00:52:07.911 --> 00:52:09.760
I say if you do you're like yes ma'am.

00:52:09.760 --> 00:52:11.451
I'm going to show up there in person.

00:52:11.451 --> 00:52:14.270
But she has that ability.

00:52:14.270 --> 00:52:17.460
She knows I've created that space
where she can express herself.

00:52:17.460 --> 00:52:20.110
And she can tell me what she needs,
so I know what's really important.

00:52:20.110 --> 00:52:23.648
So guess what there's something really
important happening on my job and

00:52:23.648 --> 00:52:27.753
I'm like, I'm going to be in person, at my
daughter's school that day, that is not,

00:52:27.753 --> 00:52:29.120
there is nothing.

00:52:29.120 --> 00:52:31.290
That is going to keep me
away from being there.

00:52:31.290 --> 00:52:36.420
Now am I at every, she has gymnastics,
am I at every single gym?

00:52:36.420 --> 00:52:36.980
No, I'm not.

00:52:36.980 --> 00:52:38.170
I'm not going to lie.

00:52:38.170 --> 00:52:40.740
I'm not going to sit up here and
tell you that because it's not true.

00:52:40.740 --> 00:52:43.160
I'm not at all her little,
I'm not at everything.

00:52:43.160 --> 00:52:45.340
But the things that matter to her,
I'm there.

00:52:45.340 --> 00:52:46.950
And I show up for that.

00:52:46.950 --> 00:52:50.530
So for me, it's about I kind of think
with objectives and key results.

00:52:50.530 --> 00:52:52.850
So I have my objectives and my goals.

00:52:52.850 --> 00:52:56.754
The things that are important to me and
I do that another one with my husband,

00:52:56.754 --> 00:52:59.620
every year we try to go
to a marriage retreat.

00:52:59.620 --> 00:53:00.710
We try to do it every year.

00:53:02.260 --> 00:53:05.960
No matter how challenging it is,
we make space for that.

00:53:05.960 --> 00:53:06.880
It's an objective.

00:53:06.880 --> 00:53:07.490
It's important.

00:53:07.490 --> 00:53:09.450
We sit there, think about our vision.

00:53:09.450 --> 00:53:10.969
What are we going to do.

00:53:10.969 --> 00:53:13.440
We plan we're intentional.

00:53:13.440 --> 00:53:14.720
That works for
us doesn't work for everybody.

00:53:14.720 --> 00:53:15.420
Not everybody needs this.

00:53:15.420 --> 00:53:17.130
Some of my friends are like why?

00:53:17.130 --> 00:53:18.330
Not necessarily.

00:53:18.330 --> 00:53:19.870
We love it, works for us, right?

00:53:19.870 --> 00:53:21.180
Do what works for you.

00:53:21.180 --> 00:53:25.301
But it works for us that intentionality
and so for me, it's about creating those

00:53:25.301 --> 00:53:28.998
space, being stubborn about the things
that matter most to my family and

00:53:28.998 --> 00:53:30.890
making sure that I prioritize that.

00:53:30.890 --> 00:53:38.017
The other things there's no balance,
like I said [LAUGH] There's harmony.

00:53:38.017 --> 00:53:39.490
&gt;&gt; One more?

00:53:39.490 --> 00:53:41.240
&gt;&gt; Hi.
Thank you for being here.

00:53:41.240 --> 00:53:42.840
You're really captivating.

00:53:42.840 --> 00:53:44.570
So it's been awesome to hear you speak.

00:53:44.570 --> 00:53:47.190
I'm different from the family
life going back to organizations.

00:53:47.190 --> 00:53:50.541
I think one of the things that my
prior organizations struggle with,

00:53:50.541 --> 00:53:54.360
especially during the pandemic is
unexperienced athletics as well.

00:53:54.360 --> 00:53:58.224
As how much leniency and understanding
do you give to your employees,

00:53:58.224 --> 00:54:01.432
in hard times and
whether that be in their personal lives or

00:54:01.432 --> 00:54:05.110
at work and you seem like you're
exceptional at managing teams.

00:54:05.110 --> 00:54:09.682
How do you balance being an understanding
leader with hardline expectations on work,

00:54:09.682 --> 00:54:11.800
showing up like you've said.

00:54:11.800 --> 00:54:15.351
And expectations that your team
display grit and determination that

00:54:15.351 --> 00:54:18.910
are concomitant with being in
an ambitious workplace environment.

00:54:20.330 --> 00:54:24.314
&gt;&gt; So
answer that question with this story.

00:54:24.314 --> 00:54:30.099
Every one is going through a journey,
even if you don't know it and

00:54:30.099 --> 00:54:33.416
you don't know what that journey is.

00:54:33.416 --> 00:54:36.802
And for me,
having constant communication and

00:54:36.802 --> 00:54:39.938
being open with my team and
keeping it real.

00:54:39.938 --> 00:54:44.268
I really tried to keep it raw, don't do
it well, perfectly 100% of the time, but

00:54:44.268 --> 00:54:48.259
I always tried to show up as my authentic
self, and I always try to keep it real.

00:54:48.259 --> 00:54:53.323
And what that means is I ask questions,
and the people that work for

00:54:53.323 --> 00:54:58.940
me that are closest to me know,
they can tell me exactly what's going on.

00:54:58.940 --> 00:55:04.430
So I was talking to someone who worked for
me at a job and

00:55:04.430 --> 00:55:10.600
the person told me, hey,
the person had a very big job.

00:55:10.600 --> 00:55:16.636
And they were like, this job right now,
for what I'm going on in my personal life,

00:55:16.636 --> 00:55:21.920
this is a lot and I don't know if it
makes sense for me to keep doing this.

00:55:21.920 --> 00:55:25.040
We had that open and
honest conversation, right?

00:55:26.180 --> 00:55:31.654
And that helped me understand
wow this is a lot.

00:55:31.654 --> 00:55:33.840
How can I support this person?

00:55:33.840 --> 00:55:37.678
And so I started to think
about how I could support and

00:55:37.678 --> 00:55:42.303
make sure that they have the team
around them to support them and

00:55:42.303 --> 00:55:47.015
I'm able to support them but
ultimately, the person decided,

00:55:47.015 --> 00:55:52.570
I'm not sure that this type of
job makes sense for me anymore.

00:55:52.570 --> 00:55:56.190
And I remember when he
called me to tell me,

00:55:56.190 --> 00:56:01.410
I was very nervous and
I would like talk him out of it, I didn't.

00:56:02.460 --> 00:56:03.865
I asked him why.

00:56:03.865 --> 00:56:06.926
And when he explained to me, he said.

00:56:06.926 --> 00:56:11.279
When I first took this job,
I worked till 10PM that day, and

00:56:11.279 --> 00:56:14.810
I haven't stopped and
it's been several years.

00:56:16.040 --> 00:56:19.090
I have young children and
family that live abroad, but

00:56:19.090 --> 00:56:21.350
I just I need something different.

00:56:21.350 --> 00:56:23.550
It made sense to me, right?

00:56:23.550 --> 00:56:25.717
And so I asked him, well,
what do you want to do?

00:56:25.717 --> 00:56:27.260
And he said, I ultimately want to leave.

00:56:27.260 --> 00:56:28.495
And I was like, how soon?

00:56:28.495 --> 00:56:32.721
And we talked about it and we worked out
a plan, a beautiful plan that worked well

00:56:32.721 --> 00:56:35.891
for me, for him, for
the organization, for the company.

00:56:35.891 --> 00:56:38.325
And I think this comes back
to being people first, right?

00:56:38.325 --> 00:56:39.694
Ultimately, I'm like,

00:56:39.694 --> 00:56:43.060
what is in the company's best
interest is a lack of disruption.

00:56:43.060 --> 00:56:47.166
Right, that is ultimate for a leader this
big, that is ultimately what is best for

00:56:47.166 --> 00:56:48.880
the company.

00:56:48.880 --> 00:56:51.220
But it's also what's best for him.

00:56:51.220 --> 00:56:54.755
So, how can we work together on
a transition plan that is going to

00:56:54.755 --> 00:56:55.540
make sense?

00:56:55.540 --> 00:57:00.300
I think often what happens is, and
by the way, I'm guilty of this.

00:57:00.300 --> 00:57:02.350
I think we're all guilty
of it in some ways.

00:57:02.350 --> 00:57:05.180
We don't always put ourselves
in other people's shoes.

00:57:05.180 --> 00:57:09.470
So, we want to do the most expedient
thing, not always the right thing.

00:57:09.470 --> 00:57:12.710
And I think that's where as leaders
you can make a bit of a difference.

00:57:12.710 --> 00:57:15.930
Is when you ask yourself, actually,
if I was in this person's shoes,

00:57:15.930 --> 00:57:17.390
what would I want someone to do?

00:57:18.540 --> 00:57:20.687
How would I want someone to treat me?

00:57:20.687 --> 00:57:22.483
And then do that.

00:57:25.750 --> 00:57:26.862
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Clara.
I don't know,

00:57:26.862 --> 00:57:30.422
we'd like to close view from the top
with a lightning round of questions.

00:57:30.422 --> 00:57:31.007
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:57:31.007 --> 00:57:33.340
&gt;&gt; So, I have a few for you.

00:57:33.340 --> 00:57:38.070
Is it better to be an RJ Miller scholar or
friend of RJ Miller?

00:57:38.070 --> 00:57:39.100
&gt;&gt; Friend of RJ Miller.

00:57:40.780 --> 00:57:42.552
See you in Vegas.

00:57:42.552 --> 00:57:46.695
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; What was the last song you sang out

00:57:46.695 --> 00:57:48.320
loud?

00:57:48.320 --> 00:57:51.404
I know you love singing.

00:57:51.404 --> 00:57:55.733
&gt;&gt; I got a feeling tonight's
going to be a good time.

00:57:55.733 --> 00:58:02.113
It's always a good day to have a good day.

00:58:02.113 --> 00:58:04.330
&gt;&gt; Tunchi or Cody Rigsby.

00:58:04.330 --> 00:58:07.102
&gt;&gt; Both, trick question, both.

00:58:07.102 --> 00:58:09.630
I love all our guests equally.

00:58:09.630 --> 00:58:12.466
&gt;&gt; Ahem, and then my last question,
what are you most hopeful for?

00:58:15.384 --> 00:58:19.430
&gt;&gt; I am most hopeful for
the future of the world.

00:58:19.430 --> 00:58:23.311
It's inspiring to me when I hear my
six year old or even my four year old.

00:58:23.311 --> 00:58:26.089
The other day we're having dinner and
we asked,

00:58:26.089 --> 00:58:29.768
we're trying to teach our children
how to have conversations.

00:58:29.768 --> 00:58:31.907
So, we have these little cards and
we ask everybody a question.

00:58:31.907 --> 00:58:35.110
And it's like, if you have to give
a speech, what would it be about?

00:58:35.110 --> 00:58:38.170
My four year old said,
it would be about painting the Earth.

00:58:41.339 --> 00:58:42.460
&gt;&gt; I know very interesting, right?

00:58:42.460 --> 00:58:43.950
Tell me more.

00:58:43.950 --> 00:58:47.499
&gt;&gt; And I asked him, later that day I
talked to him about it, and he was like.

00:58:47.499 --> 00:58:49.260
because I really care about the Earth.

00:58:49.260 --> 00:58:51.340
And I really care about the environment.

00:58:51.340 --> 00:58:55.102
And it's just a simple example
into the next generation.

00:58:55.102 --> 00:58:58.470
They care so much about so
many important things.

00:58:58.470 --> 00:59:01.135
Like I said, when I was when
I was four it was Cheetos or

00:59:01.135 --> 00:59:02.980
whatever snack they had in Nigeria.

00:59:02.980 --> 00:59:05.310
I don't even think they had Cheetos
because I don't know what it was.

00:59:05.310 --> 00:59:09.678
But, it's this generation cares so
much so deeply about equality,

00:59:09.678 --> 00:59:12.850
about justice,
about the environment, right?

00:59:12.850 --> 00:59:16.340
About things that really matter,
and I'm hopeful about that.

00:59:16.340 --> 00:59:17.300
Because you know what?

00:59:17.300 --> 00:59:19.745
When people care about something,
they take action and

00:59:19.745 --> 00:59:21.290
they do something about it.

00:59:21.290 --> 00:59:26.144
And that means that the world is going to
continue to become a better place.

00:59:26.144 --> 00:59:29.570
And that leaves me hopeful and inspired.

00:59:29.570 --> 00:59:31.510
&gt;&gt; Clara, that was excellent, thank you.

00:59:31.510 --> 00:59:38.043
&gt;&gt; Thank you, thank you all so much.

00:59:38.043 --> 00:59:41.670
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:59:41.670 --> 00:59:44.941
